WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.650
Picture the payments company's night shift, run by programs instead of people.

2
00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.268
After the shops close, three jobs run, one after another.

3
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:15.477
One morning, the accountants open the report for 6 November.

4
00:00:15.477 --> 00:00:18.040
The total is twice what the shops took.

5
00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:20.407
And every job says it finished fine.

6
00:00:20.633 --> 00:00:23.636
How does a job that succeeded double the money?

7
00:00:23.636 --> 00:00:24.849
Hold that question.

8
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:26.279
Here's the night.

9
00:00:26.279 --> 00:00:30.122
Extract copies the day's card payments into a work table.

10
00:00:30.122 --> 00:00:34.977
Load adds them up and writes one total into a table called daily totals.

11
00:00:34.977 --> 00:00:36.595
Report reads that total.

12
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.145
A program is in charge of the night.

13
00:00:39.145 --> 00:00:44.877
It starts each job when the one before it is done, and if a job fails, it runs it again.

14
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:50.460
That program is called an orchestrator, and running a job again is called a retry.

15
00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.899
A retry feels careful.

16
00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:54.187
Something went wrong, so try again.

17
00:00:54.433 --> 00:01:00.115
At minute 5, extract copies 235 payments for 6 November.

18
00:01:00.367 --> 00:01:10.315
At minute 15, load's first attempt adds them up and writes the total: 5,974,657 cents.

19
00:01:10.567 --> 00:01:15.015
And then its machine dies, before it can tell the orchestrator it's done.

20
00:01:15.015 --> 00:01:16.660
The total is already saved.

21
00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.148
The orchestrator only knows one thing: this attempt never said done.

22
00:01:21.148 --> 00:01:25.084
So it marks it failed, waits five minutes, and runs load again.

23
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:30.192
At minute 30, the second attempt writes its total too, and this time it says done.

24
00:01:30.467 --> 00:01:34.210
So what does daily totals hold for 6 November now?

25
00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:35.408
Pause and guess.

26
00:01:38.633 --> 00:01:39.255
Two rows.

27
00:01:39.255 --> 00:01:44.023
The first attempt's row never went away, and the retry added another.

28
00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:51.664
At minute 35, report adds up the day: 11,949,314 cents.

29
00:01:51.664 --> 00:01:53.807
Twice the truth.

30
00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:55.620
And look at the tasks.

31
00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:56.962
Every one is green.

32
00:01:56.962 --> 00:01:58.585
The dashboard is happy.

33
00:01:58.585 --> 00:02:00.280
The accountants are not.

34
00:02:00.567 --> 00:02:03.957
A green night only says each task said done in the end.

35
00:02:03.957 --> 00:02:06.361
It doesn't say how many times it wrote.

36
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:12.608
You might be thinking: surely the orchestrator cleans up a failed attempt before it retries.

37
00:02:12.608 --> 00:02:13.915
Let's be fair to it.

38
00:02:13.915 --> 00:02:16.788
It started the job, and it watched for done.

39
00:02:16.788 --> 00:02:17.507
That's all.

40
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:22.934
The row went into the warehouse, a database built for questions over years of data.

41
00:02:22.934 --> 00:02:26.882
The orchestrator can't see in there, so it can't undo anything.

42
00:02:26.882 --> 00:02:28.761
It can only run the job again.

43
00:02:29.033 --> 00:02:30.379
Then turn retries off?

44
00:02:30.379 --> 00:02:36.498
Then a network hiccup at three in the morning leaves the day missing, and someone reruns it by hand.

45
00:02:36.498 --> 00:02:39.741
That's the same second run, with the same second row.

46
00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.716
Picture a restaurant kitchen.

47
00:02:41.716 --> 00:02:46.093
A cook puts a steak on a plate, and gets called away before shouting done.

48
00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:52.173
The person calling out the orders, the expediter, hears nothing, so she calls the steak again.

49
00:02:52.173 --> 00:02:55.341
A second cook adds another steak to the same plate.

50
00:02:55.341 --> 00:02:56.956
The table gets two steaks.

51
00:02:57.133 --> 00:02:57.651
Map it.

52
00:02:57.651 --> 00:03:00.166
The expediter is the orchestrator.

53
00:03:00.166 --> 00:03:02.015
Each cook is one attempt.

54
00:03:02.015 --> 00:03:04.677
Shouting done is the success report.

55
00:03:04.677 --> 00:03:08.153
And the plate is the day's row in daily totals.

56
00:03:08.367 --> 00:03:10.074
Now the fix is easy to see.

57
00:03:10.074 --> 00:03:12.793
The second cook shouldn't add to the plate.

58
00:03:12.793 --> 00:03:14.185
She should replace it.

59
00:03:14.185 --> 00:03:16.777
One plate, one steak, however many cooks.

60
00:03:17.033 --> 00:03:26.171
In the warehouse, that means giving the table one slice per day, called a partition, and letting load replace its day's slice instead of adding a row.

61
00:03:26.433 --> 00:03:29.232
Run the same night again, crash and all.

62
00:03:29.232 --> 00:03:30.981
Attempt 1 writes the day.

63
00:03:30.981 --> 00:03:32.171
The machine dies.

64
00:03:32.433 --> 00:03:37.537
Attempt 2 replaces the day with the same total.

65
00:03:37.537 --> 00:03:38.406
One row.

66
00:03:38.406 --> 00:03:43.184
Report says 5,974,657 cents, which is right.

67
00:03:43.433 --> 00:03:45.928
Same tasks, same crash, same retry.

68
00:03:45.928 --> 00:03:48.993
Only the write changed: add became replace.

69
00:03:49.267 --> 00:03:51.746
A task like that is called idempotent.

70
00:03:51.746 --> 00:03:55.595
Running it twice leaves the same result as running it once.

71
00:03:55.833 --> 00:03:57.459
Three rules get you there.

72
00:03:57.459 --> 00:04:00.147
One: read a fixed input, the run's own day.

73
00:04:00.147 --> 00:04:06.648
Never the latest data, and never the current time, because a rerun next month would read something else.

74
00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.243
Two: write by replacing that day, never by adding.

75
00:04:10.243 --> 00:04:15.191
Three: make the new result appear in one step, old day or new, never half.

76
00:04:15.467 --> 00:04:20.039
Rerunning past days, to fix a bug or fill a gap, is called a backfill.

77
00:04:20.333 --> 00:04:24.721
A week later, a backfill reruns 6 November.

78
00:04:24.721 --> 00:04:30.435
The appending table gains a third row: 17,923,971 cents.

79
00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:34.007
The replacing table doesn't change.

80
00:04:34.233 --> 00:04:38.165
A retry and a backfill are the same thing: running a task again.

81
00:04:38.433 --> 00:04:39.998
How bad does this get?

82
00:04:39.998 --> 00:04:46.966
A demo runs 90 nights, where each load has a one in five chance of crashing right after its write.

83
00:04:46.966 --> 00:04:48.245
21 nights are hit.

84
00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:53.605
The appending table ends 28 percent too high, and every run shows success.

85
00:04:53.867 --> 00:04:57.592
So turning retries on, by itself, made the numbers worse.

86
00:04:57.592 --> 00:05:00.664
Make the task safe first, then turn retries on.

87
00:05:00.933 --> 00:05:01.576
One limit.

88
00:05:01.576 --> 00:05:05.112
Some things can't be replaced, like an email to a shop.

89
00:05:05.112 --> 00:05:06.783
Once it's sent, it's sent.

90
00:05:06.783 --> 00:05:11.733
Put a step like that in its own task, and keep a note of what it already did.

91
00:05:11.967 --> 00:05:15.230
So, how did a job that succeeded double the money?

92
00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.103
It succeeded twice, and it added both times.

93
00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:22.043
A task that runs twice must leave what one run leaves.

94
00:05:22.043 --> 00:05:24.289
Replace the day; never add to it.

95
00:05:24.867 --> 00:05:26.051
One quick check.

96
00:05:26.051 --> 00:05:32.860
A load reads the newest payments, whatever day they're from, and replaces the run's own day.

97
00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.636
You backfill 6 November.

98
00:05:34.636 --> 00:05:35.598
Is that safe?

99
00:05:35.598 --> 00:05:36.783
Pause and think.

100
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:40.205
No.

101
00:05:40.205 --> 00:05:44.576
It replaces the right day, but with the wrong payments: today's.

102
00:05:44.576 --> 00:05:47.444
The input has to be the run's own day too.
